> As I said previously, I'm gathering material for a paper on NetHack and
> its community.  One of the things I'm finding that I'd like to focus on
> is the twin questions of spoilers and save-scumming.
>
> I know save-scumming is something of a touchy issue; what I'm
> particularly curious about is how the anti-scumming policy came about,
> and what folks' stances are on it.  I believe I've even seen some folks
> mention that they play exclusively on NAO to avoid the possible
> temptation of scumming.

As a student of game-design, personally I think this policy is wrong-
thinking and mean-spirited. The object of a game is to entertain the
player. If a player enjoys the experience of "save-scumming" who are
we to forbid it? People call it "cheating", but who is being cheated?
Surely it is up to the individual to decide whether they feel that a
save-scummed victory is a lesser victory than one without.

The idea that games should be frustrating and that people who enjoy an
easier experience are somehow morally suspect is as ludicrous as it is
elitist. This kind of attitude is what is keeping computer games from
becoming a more widely enjoyed medium.

